## Recommended: Use `System.IO.Abstractions`

This is a battle-tested NuGet package that already provides the abstraction you need — no custom wrappers required.

### 1. Install packages

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

### 2. Register in DI

```csharp
// In Program.cs or Startup.cs:
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

### 3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes

```csharp
public class ConfigManager(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public void Save(string path, string content)
    {
        if (!fileSystem.Directory.Exists(Path.GetDirectoryName(path)!))
            throw new DirectoryNotFoundException();

        fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);
    }

    public string Load(string path)
        => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);
}
```

### 4. Test with `MockFileSystem`

```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/data/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
});

var manager = new ConfigManager(mockFs);

Assert.Equal("{\"key\": \"value\"}", manager.Load("/data/config.json"));

manager.Save("/data/output.json", "new content");
Assert.Equal("new content", mockFs.File.ReadAllText("/data/output.json"));
```

---

**Why this over a custom wrapper?**
- Covers `File`, `Directory`, `Path`, and more with a single interface
- `MockFileSystem` gives you an in-memory filesystem out of the box
- Well-maintained, widely adopted — no code to maintain yourself

Once you've installed the package and registered it in DI, you can use the **migrate-static-to-wrapper** skill (if available) to bulk-replace existing `File.*`/`Directory.*` call sites with the injected `IFileSystem`.
